                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> Is it possible to me create a temporary buffer with the current content including virtual lines and in that buffer?\r\n\r\nYou probably could write a function for that, yes. There should be enough information in `MiniDiff.get_buf_data()` to achieve that: `hunks` with hunk data and `ref_text` with reference text. Use `vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(0, 0, -1, false)` to get lines of the current buffer.\r\n\r\nA less robust way would be to get information using extmarks from the namespace named `MiniDiffOverlay`. Use `vim.api.nvim_get_namespaces().MiniDiffOverlay` to get its id. It is less robust because there will be no guarantee that this won't be refactored in the future to use something else. It probably won't, but still I have to warn here.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Is it possible to me create a temporary buffer with the current content including virtual lines and in that buffer?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">You probably could write a function for that, yes. There should be enough information in <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniDiff.get_buf_data()</code> to achieve that: <code class=\"notranslate\">hunks</code> with hunk data and <code class=\"notranslate\">ref_text</code> with reference text. Use <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(0, 0, -1, false)</code> to get lines of the current buffer.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">A less robust way would be to get information using extmarks from the namespace named <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniDiffOverlay</code>. Use <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.api.nvim_get_namespaces().MiniDiffOverlay</code> to get its id. It is less robust because there will be no guarantee that this won't be refactored in the future to use something else. It probably won't, but still I have to warn here.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Is it possible to me create a temporary buffer with the current content including virtual lines and in that buffer?\n\nYou probably could write a function for that, yes. There should be enough information in MiniDiff.get_buf_data() to achieve that: hunks with hunk data and ref_text with reference text. Use vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(0, 0, -1, false) to get lines of the current buffer.\nA less robust way would be to get information using extmarks from the namespace named MiniDiffOverlay. Use vim.api.nvim_get_namespaces().MiniDiffOverlay to get its id. It is less robust because there will be no guarantee that this won't be refactored in the future to use something else. It probably won't, but still I have to warn here.",
